  "narrative": "**Affected Pathway and Key Drivers**\n\nRaMP enrichment returned Arachidonic Acid Metabolism (KEGG map00590; SMPdb SMP00075) as the overwhelmingly dominant pathway, with all six arachidonate-derived metabolites (arachidonic acid, 15-HETE, 5-HETE, 11,12,15-THETA, leukotriene A4, and 15-deoxy-Δ¹²,¹⁴-PGJ₂) matched at FDR = 0.0 and fold-enrichment > 700. CDP was the sole outlier and does not map to this pathway, suggesting it belongs to an independent phospholipid/energy metabolism context.\n\n**Driver Compounds and Upstream/Downstream Relationships**\n\nArachidonic acid (C00219) is the upstream precursor and primary driver. KEGG reaction-graph traversal confirms it is reachable from leukotriene A4 (C00909) via the intermediate C05356 (5-HPETE) in a two-hop bidirectional path, establishing the canonical 5-lipoxygenase–mediated conversion **arachidonic acid → 5-HPETE → leukotriene A4** and validating the upstream status of C00219.\n\nDownstream drivers include 5-HETE (C04805) and leukotriene A4 (C00909), both generated via the 5-LOX branch. The 12/15-LOX branch is represented by 15-HETE (C04742) and 11,12,15-THETA (C14782), while the cyclopentenone prostaglandin 15-deoxy-Δ¹²,¹⁴-PGJ₂ (C14717) reflects downstream COX/PGES-derived anti-inflammatory and pro-resolving signaling.\n\n**Biological Significance**\n\nThe coordinated elevation of these seven metabolites indicates a broad perturbation of the phospholipid–arachidonate cascade, implicating simultaneous activation of the 5-LOX, 12/15-LOX, and COX pathways. Leukotriene A4 is the precursor of the potent chemotactic leukotrienes B4 and C4, linking this response to neutrophil recruitment and inflammatory cell migration. Elevated 5-HETE and 15-HETE are associated with neutrophil activation and the resolution phase of inflammation. 15-Deoxy-Δ¹²,¹⁴-PGJ₂ acts as an endogenous ligand for PPAR-γ, exerting anti-inflammatory transcription regulation. Collectively, the pattern suggests an acute or chronic inflammatory stimulus that drives multi-pathway arachidonate metabolism, with both pro-inflammatory (leukotriene axis) and counter-regulatory (cyclopentenone PGJ₂) arms activated simultaneously.",
